-- Passage rg3-connections-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a1aebce6-dd8b-59ab-adb5-fcb3a9c81764', 'rg3-connections-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Busy Builders of the Stream$q$, $q$Beavers are animals with flat tails and strong front teeth. They are famous for building dams. A dam can change a whole stream and the land around it.

A beaver family starts by choosing a spot on a shallow stream. The beavers chew through the trunks of small trees until the trees fall. Next, they drag the branches into the water. They pile the branches across the stream. Then they pack mud and stones into the gaps.

The dam blocks much of the moving water. Because the water cannot flow past easily, it backs up behind the dam. Slowly, a deep, calm pond forms.

The pond keeps the beavers safe. In the pond, the beavers build a home called a lodge. The lodge has doors that are under the water. Animals that hunt beavers cannot reach the doors easily.

The new pond helps other living things, too. Frogs lay eggs in the still water. Ducks swim and find food there. Fish that like calm water move in. Plants grow along the muddy edges, and deer come to eat them.

Sometimes a dam causes problems for people. If the pond grows too big, it can flood a nearby road or field. Then workers may build a pipe through the dam so some water can flow out.$q$, 'draft')
