-- Passage rg3-figurative-language-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1d3ea69f-6b74-54ac-840e-308e7bc6e9da', 'rg3-figurative-language-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-figurative-language'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How Seeds Get Around$q$, $q$A plant cannot walk, but its seeds can still take a trip. Plants have clever ways to send their seeds to new places.

Some seeds ride on the wind. A dandelion seed has a soft, fluffy top. When a breeze blows, the seed sails away like a tiny parachute. It can drift a long way before it lands.

Other seeds hitch a ride on animals. Burdock seeds are covered in little hooks. If a dog brushes past the plant, the seeds grab onto its fur. Later, they drop off somewhere new.

Some seeds travel inside animals. A bird eats a ripe berry and flies away. The seed passes through the bird and lands far from the parent plant.

Water moves seeds, too. A coconut can float across the ocean for weeks. When it washes up on a beach, it may sprout into a new tree.

Why do seeds need to travel? If every seed fell right under its parent, the young plants would be packed together. They would have to fight over sunlight and water. Spreading out gives each new plant room to grow.$q$, 'draft')
